# .trivyignore — accepted CVE IDs for the trivy fs scan (PR-G2).
# Format: one CVE ID per line. Lines starting with `#` are comments.
# Reference: https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy/v0.36/docs/suppression/
#
# Each entry below is a known-accepted CVE that cannot be fixed today
# without a MAJOR bump (deferred to Issue #38 v0.3.0). When the bumped
# deps land and lockfile is regenerated, these entries MUST be removed
# and the trivy fs gate verified clean.
#
# Strategy: accept the next.js 14.x CVEs and the postcss 8.4.31
# transitive CVEs that are fixed by the postcss override in
# frontend/pnpm-workspace.yaml (postcss 8.4.31 → 8.5.18+).
# Everything else (currently 0 CVEs) is unblocked by the override.

# next.js 14.x CRITICAL — fix requires next 15.5.24+ (MAJOR, #38)
CVE-2026-75604
GHSA-p293-qw3h-jr36
GHSA-2xp9-vwfh-vxw4

# next.js 14.x HIGH — fix requires next 15.5.16+ / 15.5.21+ (MAJOR, #38)
CVE-2026-44573
CVE-2026-44578
CVE-2026-64641
CVE-2026-64645
CVE-2026-64649
GHSA-8h8q-6873-q5fj
GHSA-h25m-26qc-wcjf
GHSA-q4gf-8mx6-v5v3

# postcss 8.4.31 transitive HIGH — fix is in postcss 8.5.12+ / 8.5.18+
# which IS installed via the pnpm-workspace.yaml override (postcss 8.5.30).
# Trivy fs still flags these because the stale frontend/package-lock.json
# (npm format, kept for fallback) pins the vulnerable 8.4.31 transitive
# via next. The override does not retroactively update package-lock.json
# since it's npm-format. When the deprecated package-lock.json is removed
# (separate PR), these entries MUST be removed from this file.
CVE-2026-45623
CVE-2026-73646